This one is a PCAP investigation. Let's jump right in!

By knowing the attacker's IP, we can analyze all logs and actions related to that IP and determine the extent of the attack, the duration of the attack, and the techniques used. Can you provide the attacker's IP?
Let's go to the ol' Statistics > Conversations. We see the largest amount of packets coming from IP 111.224.250.131.

If the geographical origin of an IP address is known to be from a region that has no business or expected traffic with our network, this can be an indicator of a targeted attack. Can you determine the origin city of the attacker?
This is where we go to https://lookup.icann.org/en.
Here we find this:
Mailing Address: No.31 ,jingrong street,beijing, 100032
Well, that one was incorrect, so let's try the next one:
Mailing Address: NO.69 KunLun avenue, Shijiazhuang 050000 China
And that one was right.

Establishing the timeline of an attack, starting from the initial exploitation attempt, what is the complete request URI of the first SQLi attempt by the attacker?
So we know an SQLi attack would have "/search.php" in the URL because, well, you're searching for something initially. The first search we see is for "harry potter" in packet 88, which obviously has no indications of SQLi. However, if we keep going down a few more in our search query, we come across packet 357, which shows a query parameter of "search=book%20and%201=1". Therefore, the entire request URI (decoded with https://www.url-encode-decode.com/) is:
/search.php?search=book and 1=1; -- -

Can you provide the complete request URI that was used to read the web server's available databases?
This one took some research for me. Thankfully, I found this link:
https://www.devart.com/dbforge/mysql/studio/how-to-show-all-database-list-in-mysql.html
Which conveniently tells us:
List databases in MySQL by information_schema
[...]
SELECT schema_name
FROM information_schema.schemata;
So let's search for "information_schema.schemata" in Wireshark and see what comes up.
It takes us right to packet 1520, where we see:
http://bookworldstore.com/search.php?search=book%27%20UNION%20ALL%20SELECT%20NULL%2CCONCAT%280x7178766271%2CJSON_ARRAYAGG%28CONCAT_WS%280x7a76676a636b%2Cschema_name%29%29%2C0x7176706a71%29%20FROM%20INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SCHEMATA--%20-
And decoded, that is:
/search.php?search=book' UNION ALL SELECT NULL,CONCAT(0x7178766271,JSON_ARRAYAGG(CONCAT_WS(0x7a76676a636b,schema_name)),0x7176706a71) FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SCHEMATA-- -

Assessing the impact of the breach and data access is crucial, including the potential harm to the organization's reputation. What's the table name containing the website users data?
If we continue on past that packet and go to the next search, we find packet 1548, where the user queries table "626f6f6b776f726c645f6462." This translates to "bookworld_db", so I doubt this is the user data table. The next several searches are within that table, but we eventually get to packet 1612, which says:
search=book' UNION ALL SELECT NULL,CONCAT(0x7178766271,JSON_ARRAYAGG(CONCAT_WS(0x7a76676a636b,column_name,column_type)),0x7176706a71) FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS WHERE table_name=0x637573746f6d657273 AND table_schema=0x626f6f6b776f726c645f6462-- -
If we translate the table_name from hex to text, it says "customers". Simple enough.

The website directories hidden from the public could serve as an unauthorized access point or contain sensitive functionalities not intended for public access. Can you provide the name of the directory discovered by the attacker?
A few packets before this, using the same method (translating the URL and then the hex of the table name), I found a table called "admin". And that is the correct answer.

Knowing which credentials were used allows us to determine the extent of account compromise. What are the credentials used by the attacker for logging in?
Well, let's try searching for "username=admin" in Wireshark. Using our logic, the last login request will be the successful one (because why would you keep trying after you were right?). This leads us to packet 88699, which shows us the credentials of admin:admin123!

We need to determine if the attacker gained further access or control of our web server. What's the name of the malicious script uploaded by the attacker?
Well, we know it has to be past that packet, and it has to be an HTTP POST. The very next POST packet is 88757, and if we dig around in the packet bytes, we will see:
form-data;name="fileToUpload";filename="NVri2vhp.php"
